      <article class="ins-article">
        <div class="ins-source-note">
          <span class="k">In response to</span>
          <span class="body"><a href="https://developingtelecoms.com/telecom-business/telecom-regulation/20876-thailand-approves-plan-to-establish-domestic-chipset-ecosystem-by-2050.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thailand approves plan to establish domestic chipset ecosystem by 2050</a><span class="pub"> &middot; Developing Telecoms</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="ins-tldr">
          <div class="lbl">Key takeaways</div>
          <p>Thailand has approved its first national semiconductor strategy, aiming to move from assembly and test toward advanced packaging and eventually front-end production. The industrial base is real; talent, capacity timelines and regional competition are the open questions.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="lede">Thailand has approved its first national semiconductor strategy, setting out a long-term plan to move the country up the value chain. Backed by a new national policy board, it aims to build on Thailand's established assembly and test base and extend it into advanced packaging, with front-end wafer production as a later goal.</p>
        <p>For a country that already handles a large share of the world's back-end electronics work, this is a sensible direction, and the early signals are positive. The size of the ambition does raise a few practical questions worth keeping in view.</p>
        <figure class="ins-figure">
          <div class="img"><img src="~/images/cap-osat-wafer-processing-hero.webp" alt="Wafer processing at a Hana semiconductor facility"/></div>
          <figcaption>Thailand's strength today is in back-end assembly, test and packaging.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h2 id="foundations">The foundations are genuine</h2>
        <p>Thailand isn't starting from zero. It has decades of experience in electronics assembly, test and packaging, a mature supplier base, and a workforce used to high-volume manufacturing. Investment is already following: from 2023 to mid-2026 the Board of Investment took in applications for roughly 880 semiconductor and advanced-electronics projects worth about US$27 billion, and the national strategy targets around US$80 billion in cumulative investment by 2050. Set against the current push to spread supply chains across Southeast Asia, the conditions for growth look real.</p>
        <h2 id="questions">Where the question marks sit</h2>
        <p>The harder part is what sits higher up the stack. Front-end wafer fabrication is capital-intensive and depends on stable power, ultra-pure water, and specialised engineering talent at a scale that takes years to build. Advanced packaging is more achievable in the near term, though it's still a genuine step up from traditional back-end work. Roadmaps and targets are quicker to publish than operational capacity, and Thailand is competing with Malaysia, Vietnam and India for the same investment and the same engineers.</p>
        <h2 id="watching">What we're watching</h2>
        <p>A few open questions will show how far this goes over the next few years:</p>
        <div class="ins-watchlist">
          <ul>
            <li>Will the investment land in genuinely higher-value steps such as advanced packaging, photonics and chip design, or mostly reinforce the back-end assembly base that already exists?</li>
            <li>Can the engineering talent pipeline grow quickly enough to match a target set out to 2050?</li>
            <li>How much of the announced capacity will be running by 2030, rather than sitting on a roadmap?</li>
            <li>Does Thailand hold its position as neighbouring countries chase the same diversification spending?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <h2 id="buyers">Why it matters for manufacturing buyers</h2>
        <p>A stronger Thai semiconductor base widens the options for companies that want to build electronics in more than one country. As a manufacturer that already runs assembly and test operations in Thailand, Hana has a direct interest in how this plays out, and we'll be following it closely. The direction is positive and the groundwork is solid; how much of the higher-value ambition arrives will come down to execution over the next few years.</p>
        <section class="ins-faq" id="faq">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open><summary>Does Thailand manufacture semiconductors today?</summary><p>Yes. Thailand has a large, established base in semiconductor assembly, test and packaging, alongside broader electronics manufacturing. The new strategy aims to extend that into higher-value steps such as advanced packaging.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is Thailand building front-end wafer fabrication?</summary><p>That's a long-term goal rather than current capacity. Front-end fabrication needs significant capital, infrastructure and specialised talent, so progress is best judged over several years.</p></details>
        </section>
